                    <a href="canon-buddhism.html">Buddhist Suttas</a>
                    <a href="canon-jewish-wisdom.html">Jewish Wisdom</a>
                    <a href="canon-mystics.html">Mystic Voices</a>
                    <a href="canon-fragments.html">Ancient Fragments</a>
                </div>

                <a href="canon-poetry.html" class="canon-card">
                    <h3>Poetry</h3>
                    <p>Poetry does what philosophy cannot: it bypasses the arguing mind and lands in the body. These poems enact Arbora's values rather than merely describing them.</p>
                    <div class="canon-preview">Basho · Mary Oliver · Wendell Berry · Gary Snyder · Robinson Jeffers · Yehuda Amichai · Kabir</div>
                </a>

                <a href="canon-prayers.html" class="canon-card">
                    <h3>Prayers &amp; Invocations</h3>
                    <p>Adapted from existing traditions — Jewish brachot, Buddhist chants, indigenous prayers — stripped of theistic framing. The form is a gift; the words are yours to shape.</p>
                    <div class="canon-preview">Morning · Meals · Seasonal · Community Gathering · Ancestor Remembrance · Wilderness</div>
                </a>

                <a href="canon-ancestral.html" class="canon-card">
                    <h3>Ancestral &amp; Historical Texts</h3>
                    <p>Exile, survival, cultural persistence, and the long memory of peoples who endured. Ancestry is not abstract — it is particular: specific names, towns, crossings.</p>
                    <div class="canon-preview">Simon Dubnow · Primo Levi · Yehuda Amichai · S. Ansky · Frederick Douglass · Czeslaw Milosz</div>
                </a>

                <a href="canon-science.html" class="canon-card">
                    <h3>Scientific &amp; Natural History</h3>
                    <p>Science, practiced with attention and humility, is a form of revelation. Empirical fact rendered as sacred story — scripture for those who find the universe more astonishing the more precisely they look.</p>
                    <div class="canon-preview">Suzanne Simard · Peter Wohlleben · John McPhee · Stefano Mancuso · Charles Darwin · Rachel Carson</div>
                </a>

                <a href="canon-companions.html" class="canon-card">
                    <h3>Contemporary Companions</h3>
                    <p>Modern works that reach toward the same truths — in a contemporary register. They are the canopy, not the roots. They belong here because they are unusually good.</p>
                    <div class="canon-preview">Kimmerer · Taleb · Harris · Pirsig · McPhee · Muir · Diamond · Davis · Mitchell · Postman</div>
                </a>

                <a href="canon-upanishads.html" class="canon-card">
                    <h3>The Upanishads &amp; Hindu Philosophy</h3>
                    <p>The oldest philosophical inquiry into the nature of self and cosmos — where the question "Who am I?" first became inseparable from "What is all of this?"</p>
                    <div class="canon-preview">Chandogya · Brihadaranyaka · Isha · Kena · Katha · Mundaka · Bhagavad Gita · Shankara</div>
                </a>

            </div>
